  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure builds over the region today and tonight before \nmoving offshore Sunday. The remnants of Hurricane Isaias lift \ninto the OH Valley on Sunday with moisture funneling up into \nsouthern areas late Sunday night into Monday morning. \n\nRadiational cooling tonight will result in frost formation. The\nremaining zones still in season are the Midcoast zones and a\nfrost advisory has been issued.\n\nBreezy southwest winds will also funnel warm temperatures into\nthe area Sunday into Monday. Highs will top out in the 60s both\ndays, some 3-8 degrees above normal. \n\nAs the system moves off the southern New England coast, it \ncould bring a chance of showers for the Downeast coast late Sunday\nnight into Monday. Rain chances could decrease if the system \nstays further south, though there is still a good amount of \nuncertainty at this time. A separate low pressure develops over \nthe Great Lakes Monday night as a trough deepens behind the \ndeparting tropical remnants. This will bring a chance of rain \nearly-mid next week. Some wintry precipitation will be possible\nin the far north on Tuesday, but confidence is low.",
